NAME
    regex - the basic regular expressions of grep, sed and awk
            (#include "../lib_regex.c")

SYNOPSIS
    #include "../lib_regex.c"
    if (match(re, line)) ...

DESCRIPTION
    The dialect:
        .         any single character
        c* c+ c?  zero or more / one or more / zero or one of the
                  character (or .) before it
        ^         the start of the line (only as the first character)
        $         the end of the line (only as the last character)
    Everything else is a literal: no classes [..], no \ escapes, no
    alternation.

    match(re, t)      1 when re matches anywhere in t (a leading ^
                      tries only the start)
    matchhere(re, t)  1 when re matches a PREFIX of t; the global rend
                      then points just past the match, so sed gets the
                      length of a match as rend - start

    * and + try the fewest repetitions first, ? tries one before none:
    the P8X semantics, which make sed replace the shortest match.

LIMITS
    32 backtrack points (RSTK). At most one per quantifier is live, so
    that covers any 63-character pattern; past it the extra
    alternatives are dropped: a possible miss, never a crash.

NOTES
    Changed from the P8X version: the P8X matchhere was self-recursive,
    which y1cc rejects; it is now a loop with an explicit backtrack
    stack. A quantifier leaves one choice point (the pattern position,
    the text position of the next try, the kind); a mismatch pops the
    newest one and resumes there, with one more repetition for * and +,
    with none for ?.
    Ported from P8X os/commands/lib_regex.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    grep, sed, awk, glob
